      "user_title": null,
      "bookmarked": false,
      "raw": "<p>Yep, they are awesome at problem-solving. I&#39;ve seen videos of ravens who can solve chains of puzzles to get a food reward - for example, the food is in a tube and requires a long stick to retrieve. The long stick needs a different stick to retrieve from another tube, and the different stick has to be got from solving another puzzle. And they&#39;ll perform tasks for the reward of an appropriate object that looks like it&#39;s the right one to get them the food from such a puzzle.</p><p>And these are not learned behaviours, the ravens hadn&#39;t been previously exposed to these kinds of tests and figured out how to solve them with actual thinking, rather than a scattergun try-everything approach. They try out a couple of sensible things and then get it right. And that&#39;s pretty impressive.</p><p>They also show Machiavellian behaviours like re-hiding a piece of cached food if they see another raven observing their hiding place. They wait until the other raven has gone away before they do this. Doesn&#39;t sound much to humans, but that&#39;s very much higher cognition which requires understanding that other birds/people/whatever have different knowledge to you and young children actually can&#39;t do that.</p><p>And the common magpie is the only non-mammal to pass the mirror test (recognising your own reflection) so far. Again, baby humans don&#39;t pass this test.</p>",
