    {
      "id": 132948,
      "name": "Silverleaf",
      "username": "Silverleaf",
      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/silverleaf/{size}/2332_2.png",
      "created_at": "2015-02-23T00:13:30.000Z",
      "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Mezike\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/m/c6cbf5/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Mezike:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What about baby Ravens? Can they pass the test?</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Haven't seen any research on them, but if my own anecdotal evidence of baby magpies means anything, I'd have to say probably not.</p>\n<p>I made a hedgehog feeding station in my garden one summer, using an old guinea pig run propped up so hedgehogs could get underneath to get to the cat food I left there, but foxes and cats couldn't. Every morning I had to rescue a pair of young magpies who were smart enough to spot the food and get in there, but panicked when they couldn't find the exit. No adult birds though, so draw your own conclusions.</p>\n<p>Also ravens and magpies can learn to talk really well. That is awesome.</p>",
      "post_number": 10,
      "post_type": 1,
      "updated_at": "2021-02-07T04:08:31.622Z",
      "reply_count": 0,
      "reply_to_post_number": null,
      "quote_count": 0,
      "incoming_link_count": 0,
